      "text": "To solve the problem of determining the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the function for different cases based on the possible values of \\( x \\).\n\nWe know that the absolute value function can be broken down into different cases depending on the value of \\( x \\):\n\n1. \\( x \\geq 1 \\)\n2. \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)\n3. \\( x < -1 \\)\n\nLet's check each range individually.\n\n### Case 1: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)\nIf \\( x \\geq 1 \\), then both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are non-negative. Thus:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = x-1 \\]\nSo, our function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1-x+1 = 2 \\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)\nIn this case, only \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative, while \\( x-1 \\) is negative. Therefore:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nHence, our function is:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (-x+1) = x+1+x-1 = 2x \\]\n\nWe need to carefully observe this function within the interval \\([-1, 1)\\).\nThe maximum value occurs at \\( x = -1 \\), i.e., \\( G(-1) = 2(-1) = -2 \\) and the minimum value occurs at \\( x = 0 \\):\n\\[ G(0) = 2(0) = 0 \\]\n\n### Case 3: \\( x < -1 \\)\nIf \\( x < -1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative. This means:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nThus, our function becomes:\n\\[ G(x) = (-x-1) - (-x+1) = -x-1+x-1 = -2 \\]\n\n### Combining Cases\n- For \\( x \\geq 1 \\) (which does not fit the scenario when \\( x < -1 \\)):\n  \\[ G(x) = 2 \\]\n- For \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\):\n  \\[ G(x) = 2x \\]\n  Where \\( x \\) varies from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\) inclusive.\n\nIn this range, the values \\( G(x) = -2 \\) and \\( G(x) = 0 \\) are covered.\n\\[ -2 \\leq G(x) \\leq 0 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{[-2, 0]} \\]\nThis is the valid range that fits all specified conditions.",
