      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we can analyze the behavior of the function in different intervals of \\( x \\). The absolute value function changes behavior at the points where the expression inside the absolute value becomes zero. Therefore, we need to consider the intervals \\( x < -1 \\), \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), and \\( x \\geq 1 \\).\n\n1. **For \\( x < -1 \\):**\n    \\[\n    G(x) = -(x+1) - (-(x-1)) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2\n    \\]\n    So, when \\( x < -1 \\), the function gives \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n\n2. **For \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\):**\n    In this interval, \\( x + 1 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 < 0 \\). Therefore, \\( |x + 1| = x + 1 \\) and \\( |x - 1| = -(x - 1) = -x + 1 \\). Thus,\n    \\[\n    G(x) = (x + 1) - (-x + 1) = x + 1 + x - 1 = 2x\n    \\]\n    So, when \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), the function gives \\( G(x) = 2x \\). The function \\( G(x) = 2x \\) in this interval is increasing from \\( -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\).\n\n3. **For \\( x \\geq 1 \\):**\n    In this interval, \\( x + 1 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( x - 1 \\geq 0 \\). Therefore, \\( |x + 1| = x + 1 \\) and \\( |x - 1| = x - 1 \\). Thus,\n    \\[\n    G(x) = (x + 1) - (x - 1) = x + 1 - x + 1 = 2\n    \\]\n    So, when \\( x \\geq 1 \\), the function gives \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\nNow, we see that the function \\( G(x) \\) takes all values in the interval \\( [-2, 2] \\). Thus, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is \\( [-2, 2] \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\( \\boxed{[-2, 2]} \\).\n\nLet's use Python to confirm this reasoning. We can evaluate the function \\( G(x) \\) over a range of \\( x \\) values to verify the range.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function G(x)\ndef G(x):\n    return abs(x + 1) - abs(x - 1)\n\n# Create x values within a range to evaluate the function\nx_values = np.linspace(-2, 2, 400)\nG_values = G(x_values)\n\n# Determine the minimum and maximum values of G(x)\nG_min = np.min(G_values)\nG_max = np.max(G_values)\n\nprint((G_min, G_max))\n```",
